 "requested_at": "2026-11-11T15:58:39.468497Z",
 "text": "The clause is **not enforceable** to the extent it lets the landlord show the unit to buyers merely because the building has been listed for sale.\n\nUnder the Residential Tenancies Act, 2006, a landlord may show a rental unit to prospective purchasers only in the circumstances the Act sets out, such as after a notice of termination has been given for a sale. Listing the building is not enough. The clause's 24-hour written notice and 8 a.m. to 8 p.m. window are the right kind of safeguards, but they cannot make an entry right enforceable where the statute grants none. Section 3 provides that the Act prevails over a conflicting lease term, and section 4 provides that a tenant cannot waive its rights under the Act, so an agreement that purports to give the landlord a broader right is void to that extent.\n\nI'm relying on my recollection of the section numbering, so please confirm the exact wording of section 30 and its conditions on the e-Laws website before relying on this.\n\nVerdict: not enforceable\nSection: 30",
